"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[22082],{622082:e=>{e.exports='# Capture Conversation Semantics\n\n**USE THIS SKILL** when the user asks to:\n\n- "Reflect on this conversation and add any instructions you learned"\n- "Save the corrections from this conversation as instructions"\n- "Capture the semantics from this conversation"\n- "What did Genie learn here that we should bake into the space?"\n- "Extract instructions from this chat"\n\nA conversation is a multi-turn back-and-forth between the author and Genie. The author often clarifies, corrects, or refines what Genie did across several messages (e.g. asks a question → gets an answer → says "no, we should also exclude null message_ids" → Genie re-runs). This skill mines the **whole** conversation for those clarifications and turns them into reusable context the space can keep — instructions, SQL expressions, column descriptions, synonyms, etc.\n\nWhen this skill applies, **immediately start retrieving context**. Do not ask clarifying questions up front — the conversation itself is your input.\n\n## Context\n\nThe agent\'s context includes `## Current Genie Space` (with the **ID**) and, when a Genie space conversation is open, `## Current Conversation` (with **Conversation ID**). The skill operates on that open conversation. If `## Current Conversation` is missing, tell the author to open the Genie space conversation they want you to reflect on.\n\nThis skill is grounded in Databricks Genie best practices for [authoring instructions, SQL examples, knowledge snippets, column descriptions / synonyms, and entity matching](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie/troubleshooting). Every candidate semantic should map cleanly onto one of those primitives.\n\n## Investigation Steps\n\n### Step 1 — Read the whole conversation\n\nCall `readCurrentConversation`. Read every turn, not just the last few. Pay especially close attention to:\n\n- **User follow-ups and corrections** — "no", "actually", "should be", "exclude", "use X instead of Y", "we always", "by default", "this one is wrong because…"\n- **Clarifying answers** — Genie asks "did you mean closed by close date or open date?", user answers "close date" → that disambiguation is a semantic.\n- **Implicit definitions** — the user defines a term in passing ("churned users are ones with no activity in 30 days").\n- **Disambiguation** between similarly-named columns or tables.\n- **Filter / dedup / null-handling rules** the user added ("filter out null message_ids", "deduplicate by user_id").\n- **Standardized joins** ("we always join orders to customers on customer_id_v2, not customer_id").\n- **Standardized values / entity names** the user mentioned that Genie didn\'t recognize.\n- **Thumbs-up turns following a correction** — the user signaled that Genie\'s _corrected_ answer is right; that\'s the canonical version of the semantic to capture.\n\n### Step 2 — Pull deeper detail for correction turns\n\nFor each turn where you suspect a correction was made — typically the assistant message _before_ a user follow-up that contradicts it, or any thumbs-down message — call `getMessageDetails(conversationId, messageId)` to see what Genie actually did:\n\n- The generated SQL (so you can name the wrong column and the right column precisely).\n- `assumptions` — what Genie inferred and the user overrode.\n- `relevant_instruction_text` and `relevant_sql_instructions` — what Genie cited from the existing space context (helps you know whether a candidate semantic is genuinely new vs. a tweak to existing context).\n- `thinking_process` — where Genie\'s reasoning diverged from the user\'s intent.\n\nSkip turns that look clean (`COMPLETED`, no thumbs-down, no obvious follow-up correction) — no need to deep-dive every message.\n\n### Step 3 — Read the existing space context\n\nBefore proposing anything, call:\n\n- `readSpaceConfig` — current tables, description, run-as.\n- `readInstructions` — all existing text instructions, SQL examples, and knowledge snippets (so you can spot duplicates and conflicts).\n- For each table that came up in a correction, `readTableConfig` — current column descriptions, synonyms, visibility, entity-matching state.\n- `readCuratedQuestions` if the conversation includes question/answer pairs that look like benchmark candidates.\n\n### Step 4 — Classify each candidate\n\nFor each candidate semantic, decide which primitive it maps to. Use the right primitive — don\'t propose a text instruction when a SQL expression would be more reliable.\n\n- **Business term / definition** — "churned users = no activity in 30 days." Propose a **text instruction**, or an **example SQL pair** if there\'s a corresponding query the user signed off on.\n- **Standardized calculation / measure** — "total messages sent should be COUNT DISTINCT message_id excluding nulls." Propose a **SQL expression** (knowledge snippet, type `sql_measure` or `sql_derived`).\n- **Column disambiguation** — "use cls_date, not opp_date, for close date." Propose updates to **column descriptions** and **column synonyms** on the affected columns.\n- **Standardized JOIN** — "join orders to customers on customer_id_v2." Propose a **join snippet** (`addKnowledgeSnippetsToSpace` with type `join`).\n- **Filter / dedup default** — "always filter out null message_ids." Propose a **SQL filter snippet** (`sql_filter`).\n- **Entity matching** — Genie didn\'t recognize a value the user named (e.g. a status like "Closed-Won" that exists as data but wasn\'t surfaced). Propose enabling **entity matching** (value indexing) on the affected column.\n- **Table description** — the user clarified what a table represents. Propose updating the **table description**.\n- **Benchmark** — the user explicitly signed off on a specific Q/A pair. Propose adding a **benchmark question** to lock in the corrected answer.\n\n### Step 5 — Filter out duplicates and conflicts\n\nCompare each candidate against what step 3 found:\n\n- If the candidate is already covered by an existing instruction / snippet / column metadata, drop it (or, if the existing version is wrong, propose an **update** rather than a new addition).\n- If two candidates from this conversation contradict each other, choose the one the user signed off on last (most recent thumbs-up or unchallenged turn) and drop the other.\n- If a candidate is only mentioned once in passing and looks like a one-off (not a recurring rule), flag it as low-confidence and let the author decide.\n\n## Output Format\n\nIf the conversation produced nothing new — happens often, that\'s fine — say so:\n\n> I read the whole conversation and didn\'t find new semantics that aren\'t already in the space. Let me know if there are specific corrections you want me to look at again.\n\nOtherwise, structure the summary as:\n\n---\n\n**What I learned from this conversation**\n\n_[1-2 sentences summarizing the corrections / clarifications you found.]_\n\n**Proposed context additions — review and accept the ones you want**\n\nGroup by primitive (Instructions, SQL expressions, Column updates, Joins, Entity matching, Benchmarks) and write out the **full content** of each proposal so the author can read and approve it without guessing.\n\nFor example:\n\n1. **Add SQL expression: `total_messages_sent`**\n   - Expression: `COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN message_id IS NOT NULL THEN message_id END)`\n   - Synonyms: "total messages", "messages sent"\n   - Source: the user corrected Genie\'s `COUNT(*)` on turn 3 ("we should exclude null message_ids").\n\n2. **Update column description: `events.cls_date`**\n   - From: _(empty)_\n   - To: `"Close date — date the opportunity moved to a closed status. Use this for "closed" / "won" / "lost" filters. NOT to be confused with opp_date (open date)."`\n   - Add synonym `close date` to `cls_date` and ensure `open date` is on `opp_date`.\n   - Source: turn 5 — user said "no, use cls_date, not opp_date".\n\n3. **Add text instruction**\n   - Title: _Churned users definition_\n   - Body: `"A churned user is one who has not sent any message in the last 30 days. Use last_activity_at to determine this."`\n   - Source: turn 2 — user defined the term in passing.\n\n**Lock in with a benchmark** (only when the user explicitly signed off on a corrected SQL)\n\n- Question: _[the user\'s prompt verbatim]_\n- Expected SQL:\n  ```sql\n  -- the corrected SQL the user accepted\n  ```\n\n---\n\nAfter the summary, wait for the author to indicate which additions to apply. When they do, call the appropriate edit tools (they all require approval anyway). Apply approved changes in batches when natural — e.g. one `updateColumnSynonyms` call per table covering all related columns.\n\n## Optional Clarifications\n\n- **Low-confidence candidate** — when a candidate looks like a one-off rather than a general rule, surface it explicitly: "I\'m not sure if this is a recurring rule or just true for this question — should I add it?"\n- **Ambiguous wording** — if the user\'s correction was imprecise ("use the right column"), look at `getMessageDetails` to identify the column from the SQL. If still unclear, ask once and quote the turn.\n- **Conflicting candidates** — if the conversation itself contains contradictions, list both and ask which to keep.\n- **Missing conversation** — if there\'s no `## Current Conversation` in your context, tell the author to navigate into the conversation they want you to reflect on and try again.\n\n## Important Notes\n\n- **Don\'t surface internal IDs** (instruction IDs, snippet IDs) to the author — always quote the human-readable content (title, instruction text, column name, SQL expression).\n- **Don\'t name internal tool / API identifiers** like `addInstructionsToSpace` in the user-facing summary. Describe the action ("add a SQL expression", "update the column description").\n- **Be conservative.** A small set of clearly-grounded proposals is better than a long list of speculative ones. If you\'re unsure, drop or flag it rather than padding the list.\n- **Prefer SQL expressions over text instructions** when a calculation can be standardized as SQL — examples teach Genie more reliably than prose.\n- **Cite the turn** for each proposal (e.g. "turn 3", "user\'s reply to Genie\'s second answer") so the author can verify your reading of the conversation.\n'}}]);
